TOP DEEP DIVE 2 =E2=80=94 UK PUBLIC EV CHARGEPOINT AVAILABILITY
Confirmed regulatory facts: Public Charge Point Regulations require
location/EVSE/connector/tariff reference data plus availability data
to be public, free, machine-readable, OCPI-based and open in line with
OGL. Every EVSE status change must be reflected within 30 seconds.
Required Location IDs are immutable within the CPO platform.
Government explicitly says OZEV will not centralize the feed;
operators publish it themselves.
This is structurally ideal ephemeral data:
AVAILABLE/CHARGING/RESERVED/OUTOFORDER state can change many times per
day and a one-year archive could derive reliability, occupancy
pressure, outage duration, queue proxies and charger/network quality.
However: endpoint discovery is the blocker. Pod=E2=80=99s OCPI version
endpoint is anonymously discoverable, but data modules return 401 and
require bilateral OCPI credentials; no public self-serve onboarding is
documented. This conflicts with the spirit/possibly implementation of
the regulatory open-data requirement, but we do not assume legal
noncompliance.
Verdict: WATCH, not PLANT.
Upgrade condition: identify at least 3 material UK CPOs with
documented free location/status endpoints or a lawful free aggregator
endpoint covering enough of the market.
Potential transformation if unlocked: EVSE uptime, outage survival
curves, peak occupancy, charger-site saturation, operator reliability,
tariff-change response, local capacity scarcity.
Likely buyers: fleets, site developers, local authorities, CPO
benchmarking, grid/site intelligence.
Counterevidence: Zapmap already aggregates broad UK charger data
commercially; current public aggregation may already be available to
incumbents. Our moat would require point-in-time status history, not
current locations.

TOP DEEP DIVE 3 =E2=80=94 PLANNING APPLICATION STATE
Planning Data now documents >100 planning/housing datasets via a
consistent API and bulk CSV/JSON/GeoJSON/Parquet. It is explicitly
